1. Purpose
The Technical Guidelines (docs.tech.cessda.eu) describe an infrastructure built entirely on Google Cloud Platform (GCP). We have since built a proof of concept (PoC) on Safespring, including two Kubernetes clusters and new CI/CD pipelines for the CESSDA Vocabulary Service (CVS). This proposal sets out how the guidelines will be updated to match.

2. Current state

	•	Safespring PoC: It runs alongside GCP, and GCP remains the production platform.
	•	Clusters: cessda-mgmt is the management cluster and cessda-svc is the service cluster.
	•	Scope: CVS in staging is the only product covered so far.
	•	Goal: Build and prove the pipelines on Safespring so that CVS can later move off GCP.
	•	Pipelines: Jenkins builds the CVS images with Docker-in-Docker and pushes them to GHCR. A successful build then triggers a Helm deployment to Safespring. Cluster configuration is managed with ArgoCD (GitOps), and secrets are encrypted with SOPS/age.

3. What is outdated in the guidelines

	•	The Technical Infrastructure pages assume GCP and GKE only, with development, staging, production and management clusters.
	•	The CI/CD pages describe the GCP registry and the older build and deployment flow.
	•	Naming Conventions do not cover the Safespring clusters (cessda-mgmt, cessda-svc) or their hostnames.
	•	The Secrets and Monitoring pages do not cover SOPS/age or the new alerting rules.

The Software Development pages (GitHub, Jenkins, SonarQube, Docker, Kubernetes) are still valid and need little or no change.

4. Proposed changes
New sub-section under Technical Infrastructure: "Safespring Cloud Platform (PoC)"

	1.	Overview and scope: status, goals and limits of the PoC.
	2.	Platform design: clusters, networking, certificates, GitOps and secrets.
	3.	CI/CD pipelines: build, image registry, deployment and tagging rules.
	4.	CVS on Safespring: Elasticsearch, MySQL and Helm deployment.
	5.	Operations: monitoring, alerts, troubleshooting and known limitations.

Updates to existing pages

	•	Technical Infrastructure index and Overview: add a note that Safespring is a PoC, with links to the new section.
	•	Naming Conventions: add the Safespring cluster and hostname patterns.
	•	CI/CD pages (Docker images, Helm, Deployment Pipeline): add the new pipeline flow.
	•	Storing Secrets and Monitoring Overview: add the Safespring approach.

GCP pages stay as they are, with a banner stating that GCP is the current production platform.

5. Content rules (public repository)
The guidelines repository is public. Pages will cover design, process and troubleshooting steps. They will not include IP addresses, security group rules, credentials, tokens or secret values. Detailed internal runbooks can stay in a private repository and be linked from the public pages.

6. Deliverable and next steps

	•	Updated and new pages submitted as pull requests to cessda/cessda.guidelines.public, reviewed and merged.
	•	Suggested order: (1) new Safespring section, (2) Overview and Naming Conventions, (3) CI/CD, Secrets and Monitoring pages.
	•	Success measure: all updated sections merged, including the new PoC section.

